WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.460
A practical ASP.NET Core security header baseline starts with the idea that headers are browser-enforced defense in depth.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:16.360
They help guide browser behavior around transport, resource loading, framing, content type handling, referrer information, and selected browser features.

3
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:19.820
They support secure engineering, but they do not replace it.

4
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:26.260
HSTS supports HTTPS-only behavior when deployed carefully.

5
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:35.080
CSP should be designed from an inventory of real application resources, tested with report-only behavior where useful, and enforced intentionally.

6
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.420
Framing controls reduce unwanted embedding risk.

7
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.920
Nosniff and accurate content types reduce content confusion.

8
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.180
Referrer-Policy reduces unnecessary URL information exposure.

9
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.320
Permissions-Policy limits browser feature access.

10
00:00:51.600 --> 00:01:00.000
Header ownership must be clear across the app, proxy, CDN, hosting platform, web server, and error-handling paths.

11
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:12.820
Review behavior across MVC views, Razor Pages, APIs, static assets, authentication pages, admin pages, health endpoints, downloads, and production error responses.

12
00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.800
The final browser response is what matters.

13
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.160
The baseline should be narrow, documented, tested, and maintained.

14
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:32.740
Track exceptions, review environment differences, avoid carrying development settings into production, and revisit policies when frontend resources, third-party services, deployment layers, or browser support changes.

15
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.900
The final takeaway is steady engineering discipline.

16
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:50.320
Security headers are valuable controls, but they work best when paired with authentication, authorization, input validation, output encoding, CSRF protection, logging, secure configuration, and responsible operational review.
